  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard or waited for the rule's first run, so neither has been checked beyond the create calls succeeding. The rule's status is still \"pending\".\n\n**What the data holds:** `kibana_sample_data_logs` is web access logs with `timestamp` as the time field. The useful fields are:\n- `response` (the HTTP status, stored as a string)\n- `bytes`\n- `extension`\n- `clientip`\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`\n- `machine.os`\n- `host`, `url` and `request`\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-31653e7f Web logs overview\" has four panels, with a default range of the last 7 days:\n- Total requests (single number)\n- Requests over time (line)\n- Responses by status code (pie)\n- Average bytes by extension (bar)\n\nIt's at `http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-31653e7f-web-logs-overview-749c0b4ad921`.\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-31653e7f 5xx spike\" is an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute. It counts documents in `kibana_sample_data_logs` whose `response.keyword` starts with \"5\" over a 5-minute window. It fires when that count is above 10. The rule has no connector attached, so when it fires it only records the alert in Kibana and sends no notification.",
